WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.420
<v Maya>Last Tuesday I watched a VP with perfect lighting, perfect framing, perfect posture get completely ignored for an hour.</v>

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.720
<v Maya>And the person everyone listened to?</v>

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.820
<v Maya>Camera off for half the call.</v>

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.460
<v Marcus>That's the whole game right there.</v>

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.160
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.360
<v Maya>Today: owning the screen.</v>

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.740
<v Maya>So what did the camera-off person do that the polished VP didn't?</v>

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.600
<v Marcus>They narrated.</v>

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.400
<v Marcus>Every twenty minutes or so, they'd come on and say what had just happened.</v>

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.800
<v Marcus>"So what I'm hearing is we've agreed on the launch date, and Priya owns the pricing decision by Friday."</v>

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.100
<v Marcus>Three sentences.</v>

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.120
<v Marcus>And suddenly they're the most senior person on the call.</v>

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.320
<v Maya>Without the title, without the lighting.</v>

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.480
<v Marcus>Without anything visual at all.</v>

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.220
<v Marcus>Here's what people get wrong about video: they think presence is the rectangle.</v>

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.840
<v Marcus>The background, the lighting, the eye contact with the lens.</v>

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.200
<v Marcus>All of that is fine, but it's stagecraft.</v>

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:03.660
<v Marcus>What actually makes you powerful on a call is being the person who says what the meeting means, out loud, while it's happening.</v>

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.040
<v Maya>Give me the mechanics.</v>

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.700
<v Maya>What does narrating actually sound like?</v>

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.120
<v Marcus>Four moves.</v>

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.960
<v Marcus>One: summarize the decision the moment it's made.</v>

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.800
<v Marcus>Don't wait for the notes.</v>

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.120
<v Marcus>"So we've decided X."</v>

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.720
<v Marcus>Two: name the owner and the date, out loud.</v>

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.260
<v Marcus>"Priya owns pricing by Friday."</v>

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.500
<v Marcus>Three: state the alignment.</v>

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.680
<v Marcus>"Let me make sure we're aligned — we're saying the launch moves to March, correct?"</v>

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.240
<v Marcus>Four: use names constantly.</v>

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.300
<v Marcus>"Priya, what's your read on this?"</v>

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.700
<v Marcus>You just pulled someone in and showed the whole room you're paying attention.</v>

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.060
<v Maya>Okay, but there's a failure mode here.</v>

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.360
<v Maya>The person who narrates everything becomes the person who won't shut up.</v>

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.500
<v Marcus>Absolutely.</v>

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.840
<v Marcus>And here's the line: narrate to clarify, never to perform.</v>

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.260
<v Marcus>If you're summarizing to show how smart you are, everyone hears it.</v>

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.640
<v Marcus>The test is simple — does your summary shorten the meeting or lengthen it?</v>

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.860
<v Marcus>The good narrator ends discussions.</v>

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.600
<v Marcus>"We've got what we need, let's move on."</v>

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:10.800
<v Marcus>That's power.</v>

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.760
<v Maya>What about getting into the conversation in the first place?</v>

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.800
<v Maya>On video you can't read the room for an opening.</v>

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:18.660
<v Maya>You just talk over someone.</v>

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.040
<v Marcus>That's exactly where narration helps.</v>

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.580
<v Marcus>Instead of jumping in with your opinion, jump in with a summary.</v>

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.440
<v Marcus>"Can I check my understanding of what was just said?"</v>

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.480
<v Marcus>Nobody experiences that as an interruption.</v>

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.120
<v Marcus>It reads as service.</v>

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.380
<v Marcus>You're not cutting someone off, you're carrying their point forward.</v>

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.220
<v Maya>The tangent problem, though.</v>

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.340
<v Maya>Ten minutes in, your principal takes the whole meeting off course.</v>

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.160
<v Maya>The chief of staff usually runs these calls.</v>

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:47.140
<v Maya>What do you actually do?</v>

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.540
<v Marcus>You name it gently and offer the way back.</v>

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.120
<v Marcus>"That's worth its own conversation — can we park it and come back to the decision we need today?"</v>

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.060
<v Marcus>The key word is decision.</v>

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:23.660
<v Marcus>It reminds everyone why they're here without making it a confrontation.</v>

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.380
<v Marcus>And do it early.</v>

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.320
<v Marcus>The longer a tangent runs, the harder it is to kill.</v>

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.640
<v Marcus>The person who rescues the agenda is the person running the room, title or not.</v>

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.200
<v Maya>Here's my uncomfortable question.</v>

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:42.300
<v Maya>Isn't all the visual stuff — the lighting, the camera angle — just irrelevant then?</v>

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.360
<v Marcus>Not irrelevant.</v>

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:44.900
<v Marcus>Just overrated.</v>

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:51.360
<v Marcus>Here's the uncomfortable truth: obsessing over your setup is procrastination that feels like preparation.</v>

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.900
<v Marcus>The camera-off person proved it.</v>

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.800
<v Marcus>Bad lighting never made anyone look junior.</v>

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:58.220
<v Marcus>But you know what does?</v>

00:03:58.980 --> 00:03:59.400
<v Marcus>Silence.</v>

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.560
<v Marcus>The people who feel invisible on video aren't invisible because of their webcam.</v>

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.520
<v Marcus>They're invisible because they never say what the meeting decided.</v>

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.880
<v Maya>What about the multitasking thing?</v>

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.500
<v Maya>Everyone does it.</v>

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.740
<v Maya>Camera on, eyes drifting to the second screen.</v>

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:24.460
<v Marcus>People can always tell, and it destroys you faster than bad lighting ever could, because it says this meeting isn't worth my full attention.</v>

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:31.100
<v Marcus>If you need to take notes, narrate it: "I'm going to take notes, so if I look down, that's why."</v>

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.580
<v Marcus>Say it out loud and it becomes professionalism instead of distraction.</v>

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:37.360
<v Marcus>See the pattern?</v>

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.500
<v Marcus>The voice fixes everything.</v>

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:45.380
<v Maya>So the whole episode is really one sentence: the person who says what happened owns what happens next.</v>

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:46.340
<v Marcus>That's it.</v>

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:51.060
<v Marcus>Summarize the decision, name the owner, state the alignment, use names.</v>

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.800
<v Marcus>Do that consistently, and you'll own every room you're in.</v>

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.380
<v Marcus>Even the virtual ones.</v>

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.260
<v Marcus>Even with your camera off.</v>

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.020
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:04.320
<v Maya>See you next week!</v>

